        </nav>
      </header>

      <main id="main-content">
        {/* ──────────────────────────────── HERO ───────────────────────────── */}
        <section class="ru-hero">
          <div class="ru-hero__copy">
            <p class="ru-hero__tags">
              <span class="ru-hero__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
              MIT licensed · self-hostable · zero telemetry
            </p>
            <h1 class="ru-headline">
              Stable HTTPS for local services.
              <span class="ru-headline__alt">Public when you need it.</span>
            </h1>
            <p class="ru-lede">
              <strong>routeup</strong> gives local services a stable HTTPS name
              instead of a port. You choose the name once; it answers at
              <code>example-app.localhost</code> on your machine, and at
              <code class="u-public">example-app.try.routeup.dev</code> when you make it
              public. The name stays the same between runs.
            </p>
            <div class="ru-ctas">
              <a href="#install" class="ru-btn ru-btn--primary">Get routeup</a>
              <a href="/docs" class="ru-btn ru-btn--ghost">Read the docs →</a>
            </div>
            <p class="ru-hero__sub">
              <code>routeup</code> is a single binary — the CLI, daemon, and public server are all the same executable. Local routes work offline and need no account.
              To go public, use routeup&rsquo;s hosted service or run your own server!
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="ru-hero__visual">
            <pre translate="no" class="ru-terminal" data-title="routeup"><code id="hero-term-code"><span class="t-prompt">$</span> <span class="t-cmd">brew install mukul-mehta/tap/routeup</span>
<span class="t-prompt">$</span> <span class="t-cmd">routeup setup</span>

<span class="t-prompt">$</span> <span class="t-cmd">routeup serve <span class="ru-rn">example-app</span> --port 8080 --public</span>
<span class="t-label">route:</span> <span class="ru-rn">example-app</span>
<span class="t-label">local:</span> <span class="t-localurl" data-tpl="https://{n}.localhost">https://example-app.localhost</span>
<span class="t-label">public:</span> <span class="t-publicurl" data-tpl="https://{n}.try.routeup.dev">https://example-app.try.routeup.dev</span>
<span class="t-label">expose:</span> all paths
<span class="t-label">targets:</span>
  /        http://localhost:8080

<span class="t-dim">press Ctrl-C to stop</span><span class="t-cursor" aria-hidden="true">█</span></code></pre>
          </div>
        </section>

        {/* ──────────────────────────── LOCAL / PUBLIC ─────────────────────── */}
        <section class="ru-section" id="how">
          <header class="ru-head">
            <h2 class="ru-head__title">A route is local until you make it public.</h2>
            <p class="ru-head__sub">
              On your machine, a route needs no account and no network: after a
              one-time <code>routeup setup</code>, any <code>*.localhost</code>
              name resolves to <code>127.0.0.1</code> over HTTPS. When a service needs to be public, for a webhook or
              an OAuth redirect, add the <code>--public</code> flag and the route gets a
              URL in the namespace granted by the server.
            </p>
          </header>

          <div class="ru-modes">
            <article class="ru-mode">
              <h3>
                <span class="ru-mode__pip" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                Local
              </h3>
              <pre translate="no" class="ru-terminal ru-terminal--sm"><code><span class="t-prompt">$</span> <span class="t-cmd">routeup serve <span class="ru-rn">example-app</span> --port 8080</span>
<span class="t-label">route:</span> <span class="ru-rn">example-app</span>
<span class="t-label">local:</span> <span class="t-localurl" data-tpl="https://{n}.localhost">https://example-app.localhost</span>
<span class="t-label">targets:</span>
  /        http://localhost:8080

<span class="t-dim">press Ctrl-C to stop</span></code></pre>
              <p>
                The local daemon serves any <code>*.localhost</code> route, and
                nothing is sent to a server. <code>.localhost</code> is reserved
                by RFC&nbsp;6761, so browsers resolve it to
                <code>127.0.0.1</code> with no DNS lookup and no configuration.
              </p>
            </article>

            <article class="ru-mode">
              <h3>
                <span class="ru-mode__pip ru-mode__pip--public" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                Public
              </h3>
              <pre translate="no" class="ru-terminal ru-terminal--sm"><code><span class="t-prompt">$</span> <span class="t-cmd">routeup serve <span class="ru-rn">example-app</span> --port 8080 --public</span>
<span class="t-label">route:</span> <span class="ru-rn">example-app</span>
<span class="t-label">local:</span> <span class="t-localurl" data-tpl="https://{n}.localhost">https://example-app.localhost</span>
<span class="t-label">public:</span> <span class="t-publicurl" data-tpl="https://{n}.try.routeup.dev">https://example-app.try.routeup.dev</span>
<span class="t-label">expose:</span> all paths
<span class="t-label">targets:</span>
  /        http://localhost:8080

<span class="t-dim">press Ctrl-C to stop</span></code></pre>
              <p>
                The same command with <code>--public</code> also opens an
                outbound tunnel, so the public server can reach your local
                 daemon. Expose the whole app, or limit public access by path:
                set <code>expose.paths</code> in your config — for example,
                <code>["/api/webhooks/*"]</code> — and only that prefix reaches
                the internet while the rest stays local.
              </p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </section>


        {/* ──────────────────────── OBSERVE ──────────────────────────────── */}
        <section class="ru-section">
          <header class="ru-head">
            <h2 class="ru-head__title">Inspect and follow traffic.</h2>
            <p class="ru-head__sub">
              <code>routeup logs</code> streams request logs for any route — method, path, status, and latency.
              <code>routeup inspect</code> shows the full captured request for any opted-in route, including headers and body.
            </p>
          </header>

          <div class="ru-modes">
            <article class="ru-mode">
              <h3>Follow logs</h3>
              <pre translate="no" class="ru-terminal ru-terminal--sm"><code><span class="t-prompt">$</span> <span class="t-cmd">routeup logs example-app --follow</span>
<span class="t-dim">TIME     SOURCE  ROUTE        METHOD  PATH              STATUS  DUR    ID</span>
<span class="t-dim">14:02:01</span> local   example-app  <span class="t-cmd">GET    </span> /api/users        <span class="t-localurl">200</span>     12ms   req_Kx4mT9
<span class="t-dim">14:02:03</span> public  example-app  <span class="t-cmd">POST   </span> /api/webhooks     <span class="t-localurl">204</span>      8ms   req_Jz6nR2
<span class="t-dim">14:02:07</span> local   example-app  <span class="t-cmd">GET    </span> /                 <span class="t-localurl">200</span>     31ms   req_Mv8sY5</code></pre>
              <p>
                Logs show method, path, status code, and latency. Works for both
                local and public routes.
              </p>
            </article>

            <article class="ru-mode">
              <h3>Inspect a request</h3>
              <pre translate="no" class="ru-terminal ru-terminal--sm"><code><span class="t-prompt">$</span> <span class="t-cmd">routeup inspect req_Jz6nR2</span>
Request req_Jz6nR2wLzC

Metadata
--------
<span class="t-label">Source:</span>   public
<span class="t-label">Route:</span>    example-app
<span class="t-label">Target:</span>   /:8080
<span class="t-label">Status:</span>   <span class="t-localurl">204</span>
<span class="t-label">Duration:</span> 8ms
<span class="t-label">Method:</span>   POST
<span class="t-label">Path:</span>     /api/webhooks/stripe
<span class="t-label">Host:</span>     <span class="t-publicurl">example-app.try.routeup.dev</span></code></pre>
              <p>
                Routes that opt into capture retain the full incoming request.
                IDs come from <code>routeup logs</code> — copy one and run
                <code>routeup inspect &lt;id&gt;</code> to see headers and body.
              </p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </section>

        {/* ────────────────────────── WHY THIS EXISTS ──────────────────────── */}
        <section class="ru-section">
          <header class="ru-head">
            <h2 class="ru-head__title">Why this exists.</h2>
            <p class="ru-head__sub">
              <strong>TL;DR:</strong> Work needs a stable URL, and we usually get handed a port.
            </p>
          </header>

          <ul class="ru-grid ru-grid--problems">
            <li>
              <span class="ru-problem-tag">[ports]</span>
              <h3>Ports you have to remember.</h3>
              <p>
                Running several services means several arbitrary ports. A name
                like <code>example-app.localhost</code> is easier to keep track of,
                and it does not change when the port does.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="ru-problem-tag">[tls]</span>
              <h3>HTTPS that matches production.</h3>
              <p>
                Secure cookies, <code>SameSite=None</code>, and SDKs that reject
                plain-<code>http</code> origins behave the same locally as they
                will once deployed, so the problems show up before you ship.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="ru-problem-tag">[oauth]</span>
              <h3>OAuth callback URLs.</h3>
              <p>
                Most providers want a single registered redirect URI. Point it
                at your named public route once, and you do not have
                to change it again.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="ru-problem-tag">[webhooks]</span>
              <h3>Webhook delivery.</h3>
              <p>
                Services like GitHub, Stripe, and Slack need to reach your
                machine. Make the route you already develop against public and
                they can deliver to it.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="ru-problem-tag">[previews]</span>
              <h3>Previews you can share.</h3>
              <p>
                Send a teammate or a client a working URL for the branch
                you&rsquo;re on. No deploy, no staging slot; close the route and
                the link stops serving your app.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        {/* ───────────────────────────── HOW IT WORKS ──────────────────────── */}
        <section class="ru-section">
          <header class="ru-head">
            <h2 class="ru-head__title">How a request reaches your service.</h2>
            <p class="ru-head__sub">
              The same binary is the CLI, the local daemon, and the public
              server. For a local route, every part runs on your machine. For a
              public one, the request hits the routeup server first and rides an
              outbound tunnel back down, so your service never opens a port to
              the internet.
            </p>
          </header>

          <div class="ru-diagram">
            {/* LOCAL */}
            <div class="ru-flow">
              <p class="ru-flow__label">Local request</p>
              <div class="ru-machine">
                <span class="ru-machine__tab">your machine · offline-capable</span>
                <div class="ru-chain">
                  <div class="ru-node">
                    <span class="ru-node__name">browser</span>
                    <span class="ru-node__role">opens https://example-app.localhost</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class="ru-hop"><span class="ru-hop__wire">https://example-app.localhost</span></div>
                  <div class="ru-node ru-node--ru">
                    <span class="ru-node__name">routeup daemon</span>
                    <span class="ru-node__role">terminates TLS, reverse-proxies</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class="ru-hop"><span class="ru-hop__wire">127.0.0.1:8080</span></div>
                  <div class="ru-node">
                    <span class="ru-node__name">your service</span>
                    <span class="ru-node__role">your dev server, listening on localhost:8080</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            {/* PUBLIC */}
            <div class="ru-flow">
              <p class="ru-flow__label">Public request</p>
              <div class="ru-chain ru-chain--public">
                <div class="ru-node">
                  <span class="ru-node__name">browser</span>
                  <span class="ru-node__role">anywhere on the internet</span>
                </div>
                <div class="ru-hop"><span class="ru-hop__wire ru-hop__wire--public">https://example-app.try.routeup.dev</span></div>
                <div class="ru-node ru-node--ru ru-node--cloud">
                  <span class="ru-node__name">routeup server</span>
                  <span class="ru-node__role">cloud · terminates public TLS</span>
                </div>
                <div class="ru-hop ru-hop--tunnel"><span class="ru-hop__wire">encrypted outbound tunnel</span></div>
                <div class="ru-machine ru-machine--tail">
                  <span class="ru-machine__tab">your machine</span>
                  <div class="ru-chain">
                    <div class="ru-node ru-node--ru">
                      <span class="ru-node__name">routeup daemon</span>
                      <span class="ru-node__role">reverse-proxies</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="ru-hop"><span class="ru-hop__wire">127.0.0.1:8080</span></div>
                    <div class="ru-node">
                      <span class="ru-node__name">your service</span>
                      <span class="ru-node__role">your dev server, listening on localhost:8080</span>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        {/* ──────────────────────────── FEATURES ──────────────────────────── */}
        <section class="ru-section">
          <header class="ru-head">
            <h2 class="ru-head__title">How it&rsquo;s built.</h2>
          </header>

          <ul class="ru-grid ru-grid--features">
            <li>
              <h3>Real local certificates.</h3>
              <p>After the one-time setup, a local CA signs every <code>*.localhost</code> route. Browsers load them without warnings, so secure cookies and HTTPS-only APIs behave like production.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Path-scoped exposure.</h3>
              <p>Set <code>expose.paths</code> to limit public access to one prefix — for example, <code>/api/webhooks/*</code> reaches the internet while your frontend, admin routes, and local tooling stay private.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>One executable, everything included.</h3>
              <p>The CLI, the local daemon, and the public server are all invoked by the same binary. No Docker required, no Node runtime, no build step. Works in CI without an extra install.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Token-scoped names.</h3>
              <p>A server token carries an allow pattern that decides which namespace it can claim: a personal or team namespace, or the whole suffix. Bring your own server and your own domain.</p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        {/* ──────────────────────────── CONFIGURE ─────────────────────────── */}
        <section class="ru-section">
          <header class="ru-head">
            <h2 class="ru-head__title">Set the name once, then run it.</h2>
            <p class="ru-head__sub">
              Give a project a name and port in a <code>routeup.json</code>, or
              embed the same settings in your <code>package.json</code>. After
              that, a bare <code>routeup serve</code> uses those settings, and
              the route travels with the repo.
            </p>
          </header>

          <div class="ru-modes ru-modes--config">
            <article class="ru-mode ru-mode--cfg">
              <h3>routeup.json</h3>
              <pre translate="no" class="ru-terminal ru-terminal--sm" data-title="routeup.json"><code>{`{
  "name": "example-app",
  "port": 8080
}`}</code></pre>
              <p>
                Any language. <code>routeup serve</code> with no arguments now
                serves <code>https://example-app.localhost</code>.
              </p>
            </article>

            <article class="ru-mode ru-mode--cfg">
              <h3>package.json</h3>
              <pre translate="no" class="ru-terminal ru-terminal--sm" data-title="package.json"><code>{`{
  "scripts": {
    "dev": "routeup",
    "dev:app": "vite"
  },
  "routeup": {
    "name": "example-app",
    "script": "dev:app"
  }
}`}</code></pre>
              <p>
                Node projects. <code>npm run dev</code> starts your dev server
                behind <code>https://example-app.localhost</code> and injects
                <code>PORT</code>, <code>HOST</code>, <code>ROUTEUP_URL</code>, <code>ROUTEUP_LOCAL_URL</code>, and <code>NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS</code>.
              </p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </section>

        {/* ──────────────────────────── INSTALL ───────────────────────────── */}
        <section class="ru-section" id="install">
          <header class="ru-head">
            <p class="ru-kicker">Install.</p>
            <h2 class="ru-head__title">macOS and Linux.</h2>
          </header>

          <div class="ru-install">
            <div class="ru-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Install method">
              <button id="install-tab-brew" class="ru-tab is-active" type="button" role="tab" data-tab="brew" aria-controls="install-panel-brew" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">brew</button>
              <button id="install-tab-curl" class="ru-tab" type="button" role="tab" data-tab="curl" aria-controls="install-panel-curl" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">curl</button>
              <button id="install-tab-release" class="ru-tab" type="button" role="tab" data-tab="release" aria-controls="install-panel-release" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">release</button>
            </div>
            <div id="install-panel-brew" class="ru-install__panel" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="install-tab-brew" data-tab-panel="brew">
              <pre translate="no" class="ru-terminal ru-terminal--install"><code><span class="t-prompt">$</span> brew install mukul-mehta/tap/routeup</code></pre>
              <button class="ru-copy" type="button" data-copy="brew install mukul-mehta/tap/routeup" aria-label="Copy Homebrew install command">Copy</button>
            </div>
            <div id="install-panel-curl" class="ru-install__panel" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="install-tab-curl" data-tab-panel="curl" hidden>
              <pre translate="no" class="ru-terminal ru-terminal--install"><code><span class="t-prompt">$</span> curl -fsSL https://get.routeup.dev/install.sh | sh</code></pre>
              <button class="ru-copy" type="button" data-copy="curl -fsSL https://get.routeup.dev/install.sh | sh" aria-label="Copy curl install command">Copy</button>
            </div>
            <div id="install-panel-release" class="ru-install__panel ru-install__panel--release" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="install-tab-release" data-tab-panel="release" hidden>
              <p class="ru-release__label">Download a macOS or Linux binary from GitHub:</p>
              <a href="https://github.com/mukul-mehta/routeup/releases" class="ru-release__link" rel="noopener noreferrer">github.com/mukul-mehta/routeup/releases ↗</a>
            </div>

            <p class="ru-note">
              After installing, run <code>routeup setup</code> once. It
              creates a local certificate authority, trusts it in your OS, and
              binds port 443 (one sudo prompt). Then
              <code>routeup serve example-app --port 8080</code> and open
              <code>https://example-app.localhost</code>. Run
              <code>routeup doctor</code> at any point to check your setup.
            </p>

            <div class="ru-ctas ru-ctas--end">
              <a href="/docs" class="ru-btn ru-btn--primary">Read the docs</a>
              <a href="https://github.com/mukul-mehta/routeup" class="ru-btn ru-btn--ghost" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub ↗</a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>


      </main>

      {/* ──────────────────────────────── FOOTER ───────────────────────────── */}
      <footer class="ru-footer">
        <div class="ru-footer__top">
          <a href="/" class="ru-brand">
            <img src="/favicon.svg" alt="" width="22" height="22" class="ru-brand__mark" />
